From: SM-G955N Ubuntu port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: do not dump registers for optional native rseq probes

Every modern glibc process probes rseq and handles ENOSYS on old kernels.
The register dump is not an exception and floods out real early-boot logs.
Keep the truthful ENOSYS return and other unsupported-call diagnostics.
This does not implement rseq or suppress actual kernel faults.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
@@ -594,6 +594,14 @@ asmlinkage long do_ni_syscall(struct pt_regs *regs)
 	}
 #endif
 
+	/* Native glibc probes optional rseq (arm64 syscall 293) at startup.
+	 * Linux 4.4 has no rseq: return the real ENOSYS for its supported
+	 * userspace fallback, without misreporting negotiation as a fault.
+	 * Other unsupported calls retain the vendor diagnostics below.
+	 */
+	if (!is_compat_task() && regs->syscallno == 293)
+		return sys_ni_syscall();
+
 	/* suppress magiskd ni syscall 434 reg dump in dmesg */
 	if (regs->syscallno == 434)
 		return sys_ni_syscall();
